      "textEn": "Imāṁ vidyāṁ purā kāśchitkausiko dhārayanḍvijaḥ. Yogadharayana swangam jahau sa marudhanvani. 38. Meaning of the word\n\nImam—This; Vidyam—Stotra; Pura—In ancient times; Kashchit—Anyone; Kaushikah—Kaushik; Dharayan—By using;\nDwijah—Brahmin; Yoga-dharayanaya—By the strength of yoga; Swa-angam—His own body; Jahau—Given up; Saha—He; Maru-dhanvani—In the desert. Hey Devendra! In ancient times, a Brahmin named Kaushik used this armor.\nAnd he, with his yogic power, deliberately gave up his body in the desert. Above that, by air, Gandharva once. Yayau chitrarathah sreebhi vruto yatra dwijakshayah. 39. Meaning of the word\n\nTasya—of his dead body; upari—above; vimanena—by airplane; Gandharva-patih—King of the Gandharva world, Chitraratha; Ekada—once; yayau—went; Chitraratha—Chitraratha; striibhih—by many beautiful women; vrutah—encircled; yatra—where; dwija-\nkshyah—Brahmin Kaushik died.\nOnce, surrounded by many beautiful women, King Chitraratha of Gandharvaloka, from his plane, emerged over that\nplace where that Brahmin had died and his dead body was lying. Gaganaanvyaptasadyah savimano hyavakshiraah. S vaalikhilvyakhyanadastinyaad vishamtah. Having bathed in the eastern Saraswati, one should go to their abode. 40. Meaning of the word\n\nGaganaat—From the sky; Nyapatat—Fell; Sadyah—Suddenly; Sa-vimanaah—With his plane; Hi—Certainly; Avak-\nShiraah—Heads down; Sahah—He; Vaalikhilya—Of the sages named Vaalikhilya; Vachanat—From the teaching;\nAsthini—All the bones; Aadaya—Having brought; Vismitah—Astonished; Prayasya—Having thrown; Prachi-Saraswatyam—In the east\nside flowing Saraswati river; Snatvat—Having bathed in that river; Dham—To the abode; Swam—Own; Anvagat—Returned. Suddenly, the Chitraratha, falling down with its plane, forced it to fall down with its head.\nIt was gone. He was surprised. Valikhilya Munis ordered him to flow the bones of that Brahmin\ninto the Saraswati River located nearby.\nHe had to do just that, and\nbefore returning to his