      "textEn": "Srisuka said,\nListen to this, O time, who holds the unseen. They bow down to the beings, and are freed from all fear. 41. Meaning of the word\n\nShri-Shuka said—Shri Shukadeva Goswami said; Yah—whoever; Idam—this; Shrunuyat—will listen; Kale—in times of fear;\nYah—whoever; Dharayati—uses this hymn; Cha—also; Aahatah—with faith and respect; Tam—to him;\nNamasyanti—are saluted; Bhutani—all beings; Muchyate—are freed; Sarvatha—in all; Bhayat—from fears. Srila Shukadeva Goswami said, O Maharaj Parikshit! Whoever uses this armor,\nor listens to it with faith and respect, is immediately freed from all kinds of fears arising from the conditions of the material\nworld, and is worshiped by all beings. He who is hidden in this knowledge, the universe, the sixteen signs.\nThe three worlds are delighted, and the demons are defeated. 42. Meaning of the word:\n\nEtam—This; Vidyam—hymn; Adigatah—obtained; Vishwarupat—from the cosmic form Brahman; Shata-kratu—by Indra, the king of heaven;\nTrilokya-lakshimim—all the opulence of the three worlds; Bubhuje—enjoyed; Vinirjitya—by conquering; Mride—in the war;\nAntarshan – to all the demons. King Indra, who performed one hundred sacrifices, obtained this Raksha Stotra from Vishwaroopa. After conquering the demons, he enjoyed all the opulence of the three worlds. Meaning: This secret, mantra-filled armor, given by Vishwarupa to Indra, the king of heaven, proved so effective\nthat Indra was able to conquer the demons and unobstructedly enjoy the opulence of the three worlds. In this context, Madhvacharya says—\n\nVidya: Karmani cha sada guruh praptah phalapradah. Otherwise, they are not fruitful, they are not pleased, they are fruitful. A person should only accept all kinds of mantras from an authentic guru; otherwise, the mantra will not be successful"
