      "textEn": "He got frightened and in extreme anger, he separated the three heads of Vishwaroopa from his shoulders. Somapitham tu yattasya shira aasitkapinjalah. Kalvinka: Suraapithamannaadam Yats Tittiri. 5. Meaning of the word\n\nSom-pitham—those who drink Somaras; Tu—but; Yat—who; Tasya—belonging to him (Vishwarupa); Shirah—head; Aasit—became;\nKapinjalah—crow; Kalvinkah—stork; Sura-pitham—one who drinks liquor; Ann-adam—one who eats food; Yat—who;\nSaha—He; Tittirih—a bird; Tittiri—a bird..\n\nAfter that, the one who drinks Somaras became a parrot, the one who drinks alcohol became a sparrow, and\nthe one who eats became a quail. Brahmahatyam anjanila jagraha yadapiishwaraha. At the end of the year, for the purification of all beings.\nBhumyambudramayosiddhabhayadhridhah. 6. Meaning of the word\n\nBrahmahatyam—the sin of killing a Brahmin; Anjalina—with folded hands; Jagraha—accepted; Yat\nApi—even so; Ishwarah—extremely powerful; Samvatsar-ante—after one year; Tat Agham—that sin; Bhutanam—of the elements; Sahasra—thousand; Tatra—there; Vrishtah—for purification; Bhumi—for the earth; Ambu—water; Druma—tree; Yoshidbhyah—to the women; Chaturdha—in four parts; Vyabhajat—divided; Harih—by King Indra. Indra was so powerful that if he wanted, he could nullify the sin of Brahmahatya.\nBut he, with folded hands, accepted the burden of sin, repenting. He\nEndured torture for one year, and then, for his purification, divided the sin of murder among the earth, water,\ntrees, and women. Bhumisturyam Jagraha Khatpuravaren Vai. The sin of killing a Brahmin manifests on earth. 7. Lexicon"
