      "textEn": "He made this pronouncement in the fire and offered it, \"O enemy of Indra! May you be prosperous,\nYou immediately kill your enemy.\"\n\nMeaning: There was some error in the mantra uttered by Tvashta, because instead of short,\nhe pronounced it in a long vowel, which changed the meaning of the mantra. Tvashta wanted to pronounce the word Indra Shatro.\nWhose meaning is \"Oh, enemy of Indra\".\" In this mantra, in the Indra-related karaka (shashthi),\nit has come, and in Indrasatro, it is called Tatpurush Samas. Unfortunately, Tvashta recited this mantra for a longer time than intended, changing its meaning from \"Indra's enemy\" to \"Indra, who is an enemy\". As a result, Indra's enemy did not appear, but Vritrasura's body appeared, whose enemy\nwas Indra. Athanvaharyapachanadutthito ghoradarshanah.\nLike the end of the world, at the end of the age. 12. Meaning of the word\n\nAth—thereafter; Anvaharya-pachanat—from the fire named Anvaharya; Uththitah—having come out; Ghora-darshanah—extremely frightening; Kritantah—directly the destruction; Iva—like; Lokanam—of all the worlds; Yuga-antah—the end of an era; Samai—at the time; Yatha—as; Yatha-pradhana—as the chief; Yath-pradhana-pradhana—as the chief; Yath-pradhana-pradhana—as the chief; Yath-pradhana-pradhana—as the chief; Yath-pradhana-pradhana—as the chief; Yath-pradhana-pradhana—as the chief; Yath-pradhana-pradhana—as the chief; Yath-pradhana-pradhana—as the chief; Yath-pradhana-prad.\n\nAfter that, a terrifying person appeared from the southern corner of the sacrificial fire named Anvaharya,\nwho appeared to be like one who would destroy the entire universe at the end of the era. The universe is increasing, and the taste of nectar is increasing day by day. Burning like a mountain, adorned with the moon's rays. 13). With a fiery, copper-tongued, sharp-eyed, and sharp-mouthed. 14). The radiant, three-pointed, thorn-charged, red one. Dancing, smiling, and playing with feet. 15. With a body, deep-voi