      "textEn": "He who is in the self, is not in the self.\nHe who is born, is not born, the universe. We do not know even that far,\nWe see the lingam separately. 25. Meaning of the word\n\nYah—He who; Ekah—One; Ishah—The controller; Nijam-mayaya—By one's divine power; Nah—To us; Sasarjah—Created;\nYena—By whom (by whose grace); Anusrujah—We also create; Vishvam—Universe; Vayam—We; Na—Not;\nYasya—Of whom; Api—Even though; Purah—In front of us; Samihatah—To the one who acts; Pashyam—We see; Lingam—Form;\nPritah—Separate; Isha—In the form of the controller; Maninah—Thinking about oneself. The complete Supreme Being, who, with his external power, created our universe, and by whose grace we expand the creation of this world, He is constantly present before us in the form of the Supreme Being,\nbut we are unable to see His form. We are unable to see this,\nbecause we all consider ourselves separate and independent as God. Meaning: Here it is explained why one cannot have a direct vision of the bound soul, Shri Bhagavan.\nAlthough God appears before us in the form of Lord Krishna or Lord Ramachandra,\nand also lives as a hero or king in human society, but the bound soul does not\nunderstand them. They do not know, O foolish man, taking refuge in the body—foolish people, considering Shri Bhagavan as an ordinary man,\nmock him. Even being extremely insignificant, we consider ourselves God and think\nthat we too can create the world or create another God. This is the reason\nthat we can neither see the complete Supreme Personality of Godhead nor understand Him. In this regard,\nSrila Madhvacharya's statement is—\n\nLingameva Pashyamah: Kadachidabhimanastu Devanampi Sanniv. Usually, there is no difference in time. We are all bound by different categories, but we consider ourselves God."
